    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Classical Chinese, the written language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, the philosophers and the historians of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, remained the standard written language of China for more than two thousand years, long after the spoken language had changed beyond recognition.</li><li>Written in characters that record meaning as well as sound, it was read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> used it for government, scholarship and literature, each reading it with its own pronunciation.</li><li>Replaced by the vernacular in the 20th century, it remains the foundation of Chinese literature and idiom and is still studied in schools throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Because Chinese characters convey meaning whatever their pronunciation, scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> who could not speak each other’s languages could converse by writing Classical Chinese on paper, a practice known as “brush talk”.</p></aside>
    </section>

