    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Diviners of the Shang kings carve the earliest known Chinese writing on oracle bones at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">5th–3rd centuries BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a> set the model of the classical written language</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">221 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">First Emperor of Qin</a> standardises the Chinese script across his empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">100</span><span class="rf-text">Xu Shen completes the Shuowen Jiezi, the first dictionary to analyse the structure of characters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">868</span><span class="rf-text">The Diamond Sutra in Chinese, the earliest dated printed book, is made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1446</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> promulgates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> alphabet as an alternative to Chinese characters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1716</span><span class="rf-text">The Kangxi Dictionary is completed, listing more than 47,000 characters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s Ministry of Education orders primary schools to teach the vernacular instead of Classical Chinese</span></li></ol>
    </section>

